{"name":"Atmospheric Pressure","hex":"#c2d0e1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"atmospheric pressure","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The pressure caused by the weight of the atmosphere above an area."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/atmospheric_pressure"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atmospheric pressure","blocks":[{"text":"Atmospheric pressure, also known as air pressure or barometric pressure, is the pressure within the atmosphere of Earth. The standard atmosphere is a unit of pressure defined as 101.325 kPa (1,013.25 hPa), which is equivalent to 1,013.25 millibars, 760 torr, about 29.9212 inHg, or about 14.696 psi. The atm unit is roughly equivalent to the mean sea-level atmospheric pressure on Earth; that is, Earth's atmospheric pressure at sea level is approximately one atm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/ERA5_global_mean_sea_level_pressure.png/330px-ERA5_global_mean_sea_level_pressure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmospheric_pressure"},{"title":"Atmosphere","blocks":[{"text":"An atmosphere is a layer of gases that envelop an astronomical object, held in place by the gravity of the object. The name originates from Ancient Greek  ἀτμός (atmós) 'vapour, steam' and  σφαῖρα (sphaîra) 'sphere'. An object acquires most of its atmosphere during its primordial epoch, either by accretion of matter or by outgassing of volatiles. The chemical interaction of the atmosphere with a solid surface can change its fundamental composition, as can photochemical interaction with the host star. A body retains an atmosphere for longer durations when the gravity is high and the temperature is low. The stellar wind works to strip away a body's outer atmosphere, although this process is slowed by a magnetosphere. The further a body is from its star, the lower the rate of atmospheric stripping."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg/330px-Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmosphere"},{"title":"Pressure","blocks":[{"text":"Pressure is the force applied perpendicular to the surface of an object per unit area over which that force is distributed. Gauge pressure is the pressure relative to the ambient pressure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Pressure_exerted_by_collisions.svg/330px-Pressure_exerted_by_collisions.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pressure"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Day5pressureforecast.png/500px-Day5pressureforecast.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Day5pressureforecast.png/500px-Day5pressureforecast.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map showing atmospheric pressure in mbar or hPa","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADay5pressureforecast.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Aircraft_altimeter.JPG/500px-Aircraft_altimeter.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Aircraft_altimeter.JPG/1280px-Aircraft_altimeter.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Aircraft_altimeter.JPG/500px-Aircraft_altimeter.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kollsman-type barometric aircraft altimeter","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAircraft_altimeter.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Storm_over_Sn%C3%A6fellsj%C3%B6kull.jpg/500px-Storm_over_Sn%C3%A6fellsj%C3%B6kull.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Storm_over_Sn%C3%A6fellsj%C3%B6kull.jpg/1280px-Storm_over_Sn%C3%A6fellsj%C3%B6kull.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Storm_over_Sn%C3%A6fellsj%C3%B6kull.jpg/500px-Storm_over_Sn%C3%A6fellsj%C3%B6kull.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cloud formation above Snæfellsjökull (Iceland), formed above the mountain by orographic lift","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStorm_over_Sn%C3%A6fellsj%C3%B6kull.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Atmospheric_Pressure_vs._Altitude.png/500px-Atmospheric_Pressure_vs._Altitude.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Atmospheric_Pressure_vs._Altitude.png/500px-Atmospheric_Pressure_vs._Altitude.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Variation in atmospheric pressure with altitude, computed for 15 °C and 0% relative humidity","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtmospheric_Pressure_vs._Altitude.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Plastic_bottle_at_14000_feet%2C_9000_feet_and_1000_feet%2C_sealed_at_14000_feet.png/500px-Plastic_bottle_at_14000_feet%2C_9000_feet_and_1000_feet%2C_sealed_at_14000_feet.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Plastic_bottle_at_14000_feet%2C_9000_feet_and_1000_feet%2C_sealed_at_14000_feet.png/1280px-Plastic_bottle_at_14000_feet%2C_9000_feet_and_1000_feet%2C_sealed_at_14000_feet.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm